Third-party licenses of the SwarmOps release binaries
=====================================================

`swarmops` and `so` in each SwarmOps release archive are built from SwarmOps' own crates, under the
MIT License in LICENSE, and from the 154 third-party crates below, at the versions Cargo.lock pins:
every crate that the release build compiles for aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu or
x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu, without the `test-hooks` feature:

    cargo build --release --locked -p swarmops-cli --bins --target <target>

(build) marks a crate that only runs while the binaries are built: a build script's dependency, or a
procedural macro. (<target> only) marks one that only that target compiles. Each crate's source is
on crates.io, at https://crates.io/crates/<name>/<version>.

Each crate is listed with the license expression its package declares and the license it is used
under, which the [licenses] allow list of deny.toml accepts: where the expression offers a choice,
the one whose licenses that list names first. The texts follow, each once, with the crates it is
from. They are the files of each crate's package, as crates.io serves it, that carry the text of a
license the crate is used under, its copyright statements and other notices (a Notice carries no
license text), and the notices of code it includes from other projects, such as zstd's C library in
zstd-sys.

The Rust standard library, which every Rust binary links, has notices of its own:
share/doc/rust/COPYRIGHT-library.html in the Rust toolchain that rust-toolchain.toml pins.

scripts/third-party-licenses.py writes this file from Cargo.lock, and `just check` fails when the
file no longer matches it.


